WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.789 --> 00:00:04.710
Thunderstorms bring wind, rain, loud rumbles,

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:06.040
and flashes of lightning.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.290
But researchers have known since the 1980s

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:12.809
that they can also bring on asthma attacks.

00:00:12.809 --> 00:00:15.020
In one extreme case during a 2016 thunderstorm

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:16.410
in Australia,

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.060
thousands of people experienced asthma attacks,

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:21.340
and at least nine people died.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:23.400
Thunderstorm asthma events have been

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.100
reported in Australia,

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:28.080
Europe, North America, and the Middle East.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.410
But researchers are only now coming to understand

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:32.610
how one leads to the other --

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:34.960
and how to set up warning systems.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.790
It’s thought that pollen is the likely trigger

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:39.080
for thunderstorm asthma.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.080
But pollen grains are too large to be directly inhaled

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:45.130
into lung tissue to cause an asthma attack.

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:47.200
A thunderstorm can change that though,

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.970
by sweeping up pollen grains kilometers into the sky.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:53.000
There the grains encounter high humidity

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.190
that scientists believe causes the large grains

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:59.880
to swell and rupture into hundreds of smaller pieces.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.670
Riding downdrafts in the storm,

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:04.430
the pollen shrapnel comes crashing back to Earth --

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:06.820
and it’s small enough to enter the lower respiratory tract

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:08.880
and cause an inflammatory response

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.880
like an asthma attack.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.010
In Melbourne, Australia, thunderstorm asthma

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:17.350
has been reported seven times since 1984.

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:19.190
The city appears to have all the right conditions

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:21.650
for an epidemic of this sort: nearby fields

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:23.760
of pollen-producing rye grass,

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.500
regular thunderstorms, and a large population.

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:29.620
Analyzing hospital and meteorological records,

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:31.640
researchers found that all of the thunderstorm asthma

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.060
cases occurred in the month of November,

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:37.240
which coincided with when pollen levels were high.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.320
Also, the thunderstorms typically included

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.860
multiple storm cells, which may have helped

00:01:43.869 --> 00:01:45.960
spread pollen shrapnel.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.400
Victoria state, of which Melbourne is the capital,

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.460
last year rolled out one of the world’s first

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:52.560
thunderstorm asthma warning systems.

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:54.610
It combines thunderstorm predictions

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:57.159
from weather agencies with pollen counts

00:01:57.159 --> 00:02:00.220
from 8 counting stations across the state.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:02.580
This gives people time to plan for coming storms.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:06.740
Currently, the team is able to forecast 3 days

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:10.400
before a thunderstorm asthma event occurs.

